#: ../../../how-to/graphics/build-your-own-nvidia-modules-using-the-dkms-package.md:0
msgid ""
"Install NVIDIA drivers on Ubuntu using the DKMS packages when you're using a "
"custom kernel or when the standard NVIDIA packages are missing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/graphics/build-your-own-nvidia-modules-using-the-dkms-package.md:9
msgid "Topics: How-to guide Graphics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/graphics/build-your-own-nvidia-modules-using-the-dkms-package.md:13
msgid "Build your own NVIDIA modules using the DKMS package"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/graphics/build-your-own-nvidia-modules-using-the-dkms-package.md:15
msgid ""
"By using the Dynamic Kernel Module Support ({term}`DKMS`) driver package, "
"you can build the NVIDIA driver for the exact kernel that you're running. "
"This is useful when you're using a custom kernel or when the standard NVIDIA "
"packages are missing or lagging in release."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/graphics/build-your-own-nvidia-modules-using-the-dkms-package.md:18
msgid ""
"We don't recommend using the DKMS modules unless you are running a custom "
"kernel for which the prebuilt drivers are not supported. This is because the "
"DKMS drivers are not signed with Canonical's key and thus do not support "
"Secure Boot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/graphics/build-your-own-nvidia-modules-using-the-dkms-package.md:20
msgid ""
"For most Ubuntu users, we recommend following the more automated {ref}"
"`install-nvidia-drivers` guide instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/graphics/build-your-own-nvidia-modules-using-the-dkms-package.md:24
msgid "Types of NVIDIA drivers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/graphics/build-your-own-nvidia-modules-using-the-dkms-package.md:26
msgid "We package two types of NVIDIA drivers:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/graphics/build-your-own-nvidia-modules-using-the-dkms-package.md:28
msgid "Unified Driver Architecture (UDA) drivers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/graphics/build-your-own-nvidia-modules-using-the-dkms-package.md:29
msgid ""
"These are recommended for the generic desktop use and gaming. They also "
"support computing use cases such as AI and machine learning. You can also "
"find them [on the NVIDIA website](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/drivers/"
"unix/)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/graphics/build-your-own-nvidia-modules-using-the-dkms-package.md:31
msgid "Enterprise Ready Drivers ({term}`ERD`)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/graphics/build-your-own-nvidia-modules-using-the-dkms-package.md:32
msgid ""
"These are recommended on servers and for computing tasks. These drivers come "
"from an NVIDIA release branch that's qualified and supported for longer on "
"data center GPUs. They omit certain desktop-oriented features. Their "
"packages can be recognized by the `-server` suffix. You can read more about "
"these drivers [in the NVIDIA documentation](https://docs.nvidia.com/"
"datacenter/tesla/index.html)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/graphics/build-your-own-nvidia-modules-using-the-dkms-package.md:34
msgid ""
"Additionally, Ubuntu provides the **NVIDIA Fabric Manager** and the "
"**NVSwitch Configuration and Query (NSCQ) library**, which you will only "
"need if you have NVSwitch hardware. The Fabric Manager and NSCQ library are "
"only available with the ERDs or `-server` driver versions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to/graphics/build-your-own-nvidia-modules-using-the-dkms-package.md:37
msgid "Install the kernel modules"
msgstr ""

